<graph edgedefault="directed"><node id="Paper:mitra">
  <data key="d0">The Impact of the Feed Rate and the Binder Concentration on the Morphology of Spray-Dried Alumina–Polymer Nanocomposites</data>
  <data key="d1">Document</data>
  <data key="d2">The paper studies spray-drying of nano-alumina with PVP-30 to form alumina–polymer granules and quantifies how feed rate and binder concentration influence granule size, morphology, and porosity. Droplet size/velocity (HiWatch), inline granule imaging (SOPAT), SEM, and mercury intrusion porosimetry are combined to link atomization and process settings to product structure. Higher feed rate increases droplet size/velocity and yields larger, more porous granules, while higher binder concentration makes granules more compact and reduces nano-pore volume, providing a basis for process optimization and model-based control.</data>
  <data key="d3">mitra.pdf</data>
  <data key="d4"></data>
  <data key="d5">mitra.pdf</data>
  <data key="d6">1.0</data>
  <data key="d7">mitra</data>
</node>
